=t-1$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(t-1){e}^{(t+1)(t-1)}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{(t+1)(t-1)}+2(t-1))\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\cdot 1+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+(t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2 \\\\  & =2t{e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & =2(-1){e}^{(-1)^{2}-1}+2(-1)-2 \\\\  & =-2-2-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479841 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479841 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479841 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479841 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479841 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "23fc0071-5d5d-57b9-949a-5e0f696a08ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479841 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j+4z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1,$ $y=t-1$ and $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(t+1)\\,i+2(t-1)\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2(t+1)\\,i+2(t-1)\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (i+j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =2(t+1)\\cdot 1+2(t-1)\\cdot 1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t+2+2t-2+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479859 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479859 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479859 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479859 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479859 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "608efbbd-2dcf-58ab-9644-3b94a19193a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479859 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2t}+\\frac{t}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479863 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,3t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68446125-1b3e-53ed-aad0-d4e0129128a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479863 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{5}{1+25(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479880 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479880 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479880 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y)={e}^{x-y}$ and $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479880 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa703c37-d0d3-5fc5-8021-cece98eb14f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479880 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,i-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i-\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,i-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,j)\\cdot (2t\\,i-\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\cdot 2t+(-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =(2t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94889" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479889 #edb/ref 1071 "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}+z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{e}^{t}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c6083028-59c6-57ef-95a8-453f1847efa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479889 #edb/ref 1074 "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y},x{e}^{y},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=4,$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =t\\cdot 0+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =4+{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186479781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479899 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f5e2cc69-b41b-55a6-ba1d-f0aa44f4db50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186479899 #edb/ref 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,i-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,j+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y={t}^{2}$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}\\,k)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186485633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1045 "The Multivariable Chain Rule in Vector Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186485631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484804 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0493dc8c-3f2a-5f14-bb7c-04f41f578eb4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484854 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484854 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921